---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { site } from '../lib/site';

const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/$/, '');
const guide = `${site.repo}/blob/main/docs/CONTENT_GUIDE.md`;
---

<BaseLayout
  title={`Write for us — ${site.name}`}
  description={`How students can suggest or write a lesson for ${site.name}: who can write, what we need, and how a lesson gets checked before it goes up.`}
  path="write"
>
  <section class="section">
    <div class="container column prose">
      <span class="eyebrow">Contributors</span>
      <h1>Write a lesson</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        The best person to explain a payslip, a market stall or a scam that goes round your town
        is often someone who has just dealt with it. If that is you, you can write a lesson.
      </p>

      <h2>Who can write</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>Anyone aged 15 to 21, anywhere. You do not need to be an expert, only careful.</li>
        <li>If you are under 18, a parent, guardian or teacher sends the email for you, or is copied on it, so we know they agree.</li>
        <li>Teachers and club leaders can send a lesson written with a group.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>What we need most</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>Lessons for the India, Europe and United States editions, built on a real situation someone your age meets.</li>
        <li>Examples from places the site does not cover yet, such as Brazil, Nigeria, Kenya or the Philippines.</li>
        <li>Corrections. If a lesson is wrong or out of date where you live, say so.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>How a lesson is written</h2>
      <p>
        Every lesson follows the same shape: a situation, a guess, the idea, a worked example with
        real numbers, something to try, and a short check at the end. The{' '}
        <a href={guide} rel="noopener noreferrer">content guide</a> has the full shape and the rules.
        Three matter most:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Education, not advice.</strong> No telling anyone what to buy, sell or hold, no named investments with prices or targets, no product picks, and no promised returns. Any market figure must be at least 30 days old.</li>
        <li><strong>Your own words.</strong> Read your sources, close them, then write. Copying and rewording is still copying.</li>
        <li><strong>Sources for every fact.</strong> At least two, from official or original publishers where possible.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>How to send it</h2>
      <p>
        Email <a href={`mailto:${site.contactEmail}`}>{site.contactEmail}</a> with the lesson in the
        body of the email, plus the edition it is for.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>Do not include your address, phone number, school or a photo of yourself.</li>
        <li>Do not include personal stories that could identify someone else, even without a name.</li>
        <li>Say how you would like to be credited: first name and country is the default.</li>
        <li>Send the lesson itself in the email rather than as a document, so it carries nothing about you that you did not mean to include.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>What happens next</h2>
      <p>
        An editor checks every fact against its source, the reading level and the rules above,
        and may edit for length. You will hear back before anything is published. You keep the
        copyright in what you write. By sending it you let the site publish it under the same
        licence as every other lesson, {site.contentLicence.name}, which lets teachers and clubs
        reuse it for free with credit and means it cannot be sold. The{' '}
        <a href={`${base}/terms`}>terms</a> explain what that means for your work.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
